\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Three LaTeX Habits to Unlearn}
\author{Typesetting Help Desk}
\date{April 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Why These Habits Linger}
Many drafts reach the help desk with markup carried over from
older templates, and the patterns below still compile, which is
exactly why they survive copy-editing rounds.
The command {\bf older} shows up in bold runs, the string "modern"
arrives with straight quotes, and pauses get typed as three dots ... in running text.
A second quotation, "as is", usually rides along in the same draft.

\section{What We Recommend}
Bold emphasis should go through the text font commands rather than
the old two-letter switches, quotation marks should use the
directional forms, and ellipses should be set with the dedicated
command so the spacing between the dots comes out even.

\section{Showcase for the Tutorial}
The block below is projected during the tutorial exactly as printed
here. Students copy the bad lines, watch them compile, and then
correct them in their own scratch files.

\begin{verbatim}
% Examples of what NOT to do, shown to students:
Bad:  {\bf bold text}
Bad:  "straight quotes"
Bad:  trailing dots ...
Good: \textbf{bold text}
\end{verbatim}

\section{Wrap-Up}
None of these habits will stop a build, which is why the showcase
matters: seeing the output side by side is what changes behaviour.
\end{document}

